# converted from 09-her-voice.md
@title Her Voice
@bg depths
@mood grief

narrate He took the boy north on the ninth day, because the boy asked, and because Cael had been waiting eleven weeks for the question and had not expected it to come from the boy first.
narrate They walked four days. Bryn stopped at the third, at the edge of the dry ground, and said this is where I stop — and Cael looked at him and understood that this time it was not cowardice, and did not say anything, because there was nothing to say that would not have been cruel.
narrate So it was Cael, and the boy, and the dog.
narrate The dog had come the whole way. It had also, Cael noticed, positioned itself at a very precise distance from the hole at all times, and was now sitting at that distance with an expression of profound editorial judgement.
narrate He liked the dog. He had not liked anything in eleven years and he liked the dog.
narrate He explained it the way he explained everything: slowly, precisely, with the patience of a man who has spent his life telling people things they do not want to hear.
narrate The deep places answer. The price is memory. Not what you want — what you have.
narrate He told the boy about the four years. He told him about the winter when Ilsa was four and made him a crown out of reeds, and about how he knew about that winter only because Maeve had told him about it once, and how he had a story about his own daughter's happiness that he had got second-hand.
corin neutral I don't remember her voice,
corin neutral I know what she said. I know the words. I cannot hear them.
narrate The boy looked at the hole for a long time.
corin neutral Tell me her voice first.
narrate Cael went very still.
narrate It was, he would think later, the single most disorienting moment of his life — more disorienting than the fire, more than the mark, more than standing at the hole four years ago asking for later. He had spent eleven weeks constructing a moment, and he had built it out of eleven years of understanding of what people are, and the boy had just walked straight through the wall of it.
bryn neutral That's not—
corin neutral You said you can't hear it.
corin neutral If I'm going to spend mine, I want to know what the currency is. Tell me what she sounded like. And then I'll decide.
narrate Cael laughed.
narrate He had not laughed in twelve years. Not once — not at the Coven, not at the cushion, not at anything. He had made a decision in the reeds outside his burning house that he would not be anything at all, because anything at all is a handle, and he had kept it, and the keeping of it had cost him more than the mark ever did.
narrate And a nineteen-year-old soldier standing at the edge of a hole in a field made him laugh like a man who has been outmanoeuvred by something he did not know existed.
bryn neutral You,
bryn neutral You absolute—
corin neutral Tell me.
narrate It took an hour.
narrate He got it wrong four times.
narrate He said she sounded like her mother, and then remembered that Ilsa had never met her mother, and had to sit down on the grass for a moment.
narrate He said she had a lisp, and then remembered that the lisp had gone by six, and that he knew that because she had been proud of losing it, and that he could not remember the day she lost it.
narrate He said she sang, and then could not remember a single song. Not one. Twelve years of walking past this hole and he had brought her the cushion every year and he could not remember one song she had ever sung.
narrate And then he said:
bryn neutral She said Da.
narrate He stopped.
corin neutral Not Dada. Not Father. Da, always, the whole of her life — even at seven when she was too old for it, even when she was angry with me. Da, like it was one word and the word was mine.
narrate He looked at the grass.
corin neutral Yes,
corin neutral That's it. That's — that's the whole of it. That's all I have.
narrate And the boy stood there for a moment.
corin neutral Thank you.
narrate Cael had been thanked eleven times in twelve years, and every one of them had been for a thing he had done. Nobody had ever thanked him for a thing he had said — for handing over the last object he owned in the world, the last thing that was hers, the only thing the mark had not been able to reach.
narrate Nobody had ever thanked him for anything that cost him.
narrate He sat on the grass at the edge of the hole in the field and put his head in his hands, and the dog came and sat against his leg, and the boy did not say anything, which was the correct thing, which was the thing Cael would have told him to do if he had been capable of speech.
narrate He did not take the mark that day.
narrate He told Cael he would. He said it at the end of the hour, and Cael nodded and said when you're ready, and they walked back south together.
narrate And Cael walked beside a boy he had spent eleven weeks arranging, and did the arithmetic one more time, and got the same answer he had got in the trees at Sallow's Rest, and in the reeds at the Hollow, and on four hundred other occasions.
